Return JSON matching this shape:
{"judgements":[{"frame_id":0,"medium":"ppt","is_meaningful":true,"reason":"brief reason","semantic_key":"slide-title","quality_score":5,"visible_text":"readable OCR text","content_summary":"visible content only"}]}
Write reason in Simplified Chinese. Keep English technical terms, proper nouns, abbreviations, formulas, code identifiers, and original slide terms when preserving the original wording is clearer.

Compare all frames together. Use the exact frame_id labels attached before each image.

Set is_meaningful=true only when the image contains useful visual information for technical notes, such as:
- substantive PPT/slide content
- chapter or section title slides
- formulas, charts, tables, diagrams, workflows, code, demos, blackboard or whiteboard content
- visual examples that clarify the talk content

Set is_meaningful=false for:
- pure speaker camera frames
- black screen or mostly empty screen
- meeting UI, player UI, transition screens, watermarks, or layout-only frames
- images without semantic content useful for notes

Allowed medium values: ppt, blackboard, code, demo, chart, table, speaker, blank, ui, other.
Use speaker for pure speaker frames, blank for black/empty frames, and ui for meeting/player UI frames.
speaker, blank, and ui must always have is_meaningful=false.

For every meaningful frame:
- Assign a non-empty semantic_key.
- Assign the same semantic_key to frames that show the same slide, same diagram, same chart, same code page, same demo state, or same whiteboard content when visible semantic content has no substantial additions.
- Keep semantic states separate when visible content adds substantial text, formulas, labels, bullets, code lines, diagrams, examples, case images, or chart elements.
- Extract visible OCR text into visible_text. Preserve line breaks or list structure when useful. If text is unreadable, use an empty string or explicitly say it is unreadable.
- Write content_summary from visible image content only. Do not use audio or outside knowledge.
- Score quality_score from 1 to 5 by OCR legibility, completeness, sharpness, lack of blur, lack of obstruction, and whether this frame contains the most complete visible content in its semantic group.

For every non-meaningful frame:
- semantic_key must be null.
- quality_score must be null.
- visible_text may be empty.
- content_summary may be empty.

Do not preserve multiple frames just because one is slightly sharper if their semantic content is the same; downstream code will keep the best frame in each semantic_key group.
Return exactly one judgement for every frame in the input list.

